  "query_preview": "..\n\n- Minimal Impacts Through Tuesday: Periodic light precipitation\n  and breezy westerly winds.\n\n- Wednesday and Thursday Fog Potential: Strong high pressure\n  increasing likelihood of valley inversions.\n\n- Wet and Mild Weekend: A Pacific system will tap into \n  atmospheric river moisture beginning late Friday.\n\nThrough Tuesday Night: A zonal-to-southwesterly flow remains \nestablished across the Northern Rockies today through Tuesday.\nThe next Pacific disturbance will move across the region overnight \nthrough late Tuesday, bringing widespread precipitation across\nnorth-central Idaho into northwest Montana. A warm airmass will\nkeep snow levels above 6,000 feet, with valley precipitation\nfalling as rain. Westerly winds will trend higher Tuesday, with \nvalley locations seeing afternoon gusts of 15 to 25 mph. Mountain\ngusts will reach 30-45 mph along the divide. Overall this system\nwill bring minimal impacts for this time of year.\n\nWednesday through Sunday: Ensemble cluster analysis suggests high\nconfidence (>90% agreement) for strong upper-level ridging across\nthe Intermountain West Wednesday into Thursday. Strong warm air\nadvection in the mid-levels, along with subsidence warming, will\nbring stable conditions. This pattern suggests valley inversions\nwill develop, but confidence on the strength of these inversions\nremains a forecast challenge. Valley high temperatures during \nthis timeframe will be dependent on the development of overnight \nvalley fog and/or stratus Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. \nModel guidance suggests abundant mid-high level cloud cover may\nact to limit overnight radiational cooling, helping to reduce the\nwidespread nature of valley fog and stratus development. Overall,\nareas north of I-90 may be the focus of stratus and fog\ndevelopment as warm lake water on Flathead Lake increases water\nvapor within the boundary layer. Should widespread fog and stratus\ndevelop, high temperatures would be several degrees colder than\nforecast in these areas. Those heading into the higher elevations\nwill experience warm temperatures, especially for this time of\nyear. \n\nBy Friday into the weekend, ensemble guidance is in excellent \nagreement that the ridge axis will migrate eastward. This shifts \nthe Northern Rockies into a southwesterly flow regime, which will \neffectively mix out any lingering valley inversions. Simultaneously,\nthis southwest flow will tap into an Atmospheric River impacting \nthe West Coast, advecting a plume of anomalous moisture into the \nInland Northwest. Snow levels will remain notably high through the\nweekend, generally holding between 6,000 and 7,000 feet. Overall \nprecipitation amounts appear light to moderate in the mountains \nand very light in the valleys as much of the precipitation is \nintercepted by the Cascades.",
